Fixed, both asks. Commit f95ed76. Migrations step should work on the EC2 stack now.

MECHANISM, because it is not quite a hardcode and that matters for where else to look: the code read svc.get('launchType') or 'FARGATE', and the fallback was wrong exactly when it fired. launchType and capacityProviderStrategy are mutually exclusive on an ECS service, and rc renders EC2 services WITH a capacity provider strategy -- so describe_services returns no launchType for them at all, and the default kicked in for the one case it could not serve.

resolve_run_launch() now decides in order:
  1. the service's own capacityProviderStrategy -- your ask #2, and better than naming the provider ourselves: the one-off lands on the same ASG the services use AND engages that provider's managed scaling, which a bare launchType=EC2 would not;
  2. the service's own launchType;
  3. the task def's requiresCompatibilities, preferring FARGATE when it declares both -- your ask #3, satisfied naturally since the service's own setting always wins first;
  4. nothing, deferring to the cluster's default capacity provider strategy rather than guessing a launch type ECS may reject.
Never emits both keys (RunTask rejects that). Ask #1 holds: Fargate stacks are byte-for-byte unchanged, asserted by test.

PLAN-TIME CHECK: implemented, but aimed at the hazard that SURVIVES the fix rather than the one it removes -- flagging 'this stack has EC2 services and uses rc run' is no longer a warning worth giving, because it now works. What does not go away: on Fargate a one-off always has somewhere to run, but on EC2 it needs a free slot on an instance that already exists, and auto_size models declared SERVICES only. A full fleet leaves a migrate-before-roll task PENDING while the ASG boots -- same outcome you were protecting against (a deploy that cannot safely ship a migration), different cause. A mode: task lifecycle hook on an EC2 service is statically detectable, so rc now warns at plan time naming the hooks and the actual slack (fleet task slots vs declared tasks), and points at mode: exec as the alternative that needs no new slot.

NOTE this only sees hooks declared in rc.yml. 'rc run' invoked directly from a workflow step is outside rc's view -- if you want those covered, moving them to mode: task hooks would both surface them here and put them under the same versioned config as the rest of the deploy.
